The MCP server now offers a broad set of tools for AI agents: reading and searching content, creating, editing and publishing pages, managing constructs, and administering users and groups. Agents can also create folders, and upload files from a URL, which the CMS fetches only from the hosts configured in @mcp.upload_url_hosts@ (environment variable @MCP_UPLOAD_URL_HOSTS@). With multichannelling, the write tools act in the node or channel given as @nodeId@, and refuse a page the channel only inherits. The tool @whoami@ also returns the CMS version, and the base URL configured as @mcp.base_url@ (environment variable @MCP_BASE_URL@). Every tool checks the same permissions as the corresponding REST API call. A failed tool call now also returns a structured @problem@ (type, status, whether a retry may help, and the CMS response code and messages), next to its error text, so that a client can tell for example a page locked by another user from a missing permission.
